      'The church traces itself to Peter and the church of Antioch. Syriac, a dialect of Aramaic, became the language of a rich Christian culture centered on Edessa, which produced the Peshitta Bible and the hymns of Ephrem the Syrian (d. 373).',
      'After Chalcedon, the leading anti-Chalcedonian theologian Severus became patriarch of Antioch (512) but was deposed in 518. Under Emperor Justinian, bishops who rejected Chalcedon were removed. With the help of Empress Theodora, Jacob Baradaeus was secretly consecrated bishop (c. 542) and spent decades in disguise ordaining bishops and priests, preserving the church’s hierarchy.',
